  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 315 PM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n===== Tuesday - Saturday =====\n\nWith the return to southerly flow and later southwesterly flow at \nthe surface Tuesday, hot and muggy air returns at least briefly \nTuesday and Wednesday afternoon as the parent high to our north \ndrifts towards the east. A lower level jet from an approaching \ntrough upstream will help bring in air from the Gulf with highs \nTuesday and Wednesday reaching the low to mid 90s. Apparent \ntemperatures Wednesday may reach the low 100s again just for one day.\n\nThe upstream trough will trek across southern Canada bringing a \ntrailing cold front in its wake. The timing of this front looks to \nhold off until Thursday morning to midday Thursday. Ahead of the \nfront, moisture will be streaming in. Aided by the lower level jet, \nthere may be enough forcing for storms Thursday. However, lapse \nrates may struggle along with CAPE values struggling to get near \n1,000 J/kg. This may change though because stability could decrease \nif the front waits to approach later in the afternoon. Rainfall \ntotals look to be light, though LREF solutions are right-skewed with \na tail approaching 1.5\" inches. Altogether, widespread rain will \nmove across the area along a stalling out front. High temperatures \nare highly uncertain, with model guidance ranging from the mid 70s \nto low 90s possible -- dependent on the amount of rain and timing of \nthe front.\n\nWe close the week out on Friday and Saturday with drying skies \nand temperatures a bit cooler in the mid to upper 80s once again.",
